// A recipe, written from md-docs/recipes/translate-by-the-words.md by scripts/docsite/recipes.py;
// edit the recipe, not this file.
page RecipeTranslateByTheWords(path: "/recipes/translate-by-the-words", title: "Translate by the words as written — Recipes", description: "Translate a project written in one language without inventing keys: with literals on, each string is looked up by its own words.") {
    RecipeShell("Translate by the words as written", summary: "Translate a project that was written in one language from the start, without inventing keys or wrapping every string in `t()`: each string a reader sees is looked up in the reader's table by its own words. Turn it on with `\"literals\": true` in the `i18n` config:", outputs: ["website", "app", "desktop"], since: "", recipes: RECIPE_INDEX, sections: [Anchor(id: "how-it-works", label: "How it works")], related: [RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Publish a page in each language", to: "/recipes/site-in-two-languages"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Make a site work offline", to: "/recipes/work-offline"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Keep writes made offline", to: "/recipes/keep-writes-made-offline")], source: "md-docs/recipes/translate-by-the-words.md", how: true, hasVariations: false, project: { "src/pages/TranslateByTheWords.wf": #"page Problems(path: "/", title: "Problems", description: "What the checks found.") {
    state report = { count: 3, files: 11 }
    Heading("Problems").h1              // المشاكل, for a reader in ar
    Text("{report.count} missing")      // one key, `{count} missing`
    Text("{report.files} files")        // 11 ملفًا, for a reader in ar
}
"#, "webfluent.app.json": #"{
  "name": "translate-by-the-words",
  "i18n": {
    "default_locale": "en",
    "locales": [
      "en",
      "ar"
    ],
    "literals": true
  }
}
"# }) {
        code {
            Markdown("**Needs:** the `i18n` setting below, and the translation files [How it works](#how-it-works) shows. It claims `/`: paste it into a new project, or replace the starter's `src/pages/Home.wf` with it.")
            CodeBlock(#"{ "i18n": { "default_locale": "en", "locales": ["en", "ar"], "literals": true } }"#, title: "json")
            Markdown("This page keeps its English words, and a reader in Arabic sees them translated:")
            CodeBlock(#"page Problems(path: "/", title: "Problems", description: "What the checks found.") {
    state report = { count: 3, files: 11 }
    Heading("Problems").h1              // المشاكل, for a reader in ar
    Text("{report.count} missing")      // one key, `{count} missing`
    Text("{report.files} files")        // 11 ملفًا, for a reader in ar
}"#, title: "wf")
        }
        Markdown("Every string a reader sees is looked up by its text as written: an element's text, and props such as `label:`, `title:`, `placeholder:`, `aria-label:` and `alt:`. The Arabic table, `src/translations/ar.json`, maps the English words to their translation:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "Problems": "المشاكل",
  "{count} missing": "{count} مفقودة",
  "{files} files.zero": "لا توجد ملفات",
  "{files} files.one": "ملف واحد",
  "{files} files.two": "ملفان",
  "{files} files.few": "{files} ملفات",
  "{files} files.many": "{files} ملفًا",
  "{files} files.other": "{files} ملف"
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("How the text becomes a key:\n\n- **A splice of a plain path is a placeholder.** It is named for the path's last part, so `\"\{report.count\} missing\"` is the key `\{count\} missing`. A splice of anything more, such as `\"\{n + 1\} more\"`, keeps the text as written.\n- **Each branch of an `if` is its own key** when the text chooses between them.\n- **A value shown by its name** — a `derived` or `state` — is looked up only when its name says it is words, such as `countText` or `emptyLabel`. Shown words that neither rule covers draw an `I05` warning.\n- **What is not words is left alone:** an icon's name, `Code(…)`, and a component named `Kbd`.\n- **A list of words is translated word by word.** A `labels:` prop, which holds one word for each option of a control whose options are values, is looked up item by item: `Segmented(options: [\"2\", \"4\"], labels: [\"2 spaces\", \"4 spaces\"])` on your own `Segmented` component.\n\nThe default locale's messages are the words themselves, so its file may be empty. `wf translations` counts each string as a key in use, and lists what another locale has not translated as missing.\n\nAn empty `en.json` shows `\{files\} files` as written, so an English reader sees `11 files`, and `1 files` for one. To get \"1 file\" right, give `en.json` its two forms:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "{files} files.one": "{files} file", "{files} files.other": "{files} files" }"#, title: "json")

        Heading("Count by the number in the text", id: "count-by-the-number-in-the-text").h3

        Markdown("A literal with one number spliced into it picks its plural form by that number, as `\"\{report.files\} files\"` does. The key's plural forms are keys too, as for `count` with `t()`: `\{files\} files.one`, `\{files\} files.other` and the rest, as in the table above.\n\nThe default locale gives its forms the same way — `\"\{files\} files.one\": \"\{files\} file\"` — where its words differ by count. With two numbers in the text, neither is taken as the count: the message is looked up as written.")

    }
}
